## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to decompose a 128‑bit value into four 32‑bit lanes and re‑assemble them without branches.

## Key Concepts
- Lane extraction via masks and shifts
- 128‑bit as hi/lo pairs with 32‑bit lanes
- Branchless permutation and safe packing

## Code Example
```javascript
uint128 permute32x4_128(uint128 x) {
  uint32 A = (uint32)(x.hi >> 32);
  uint32 B = (uint32)(x.hi & 0xFFFFFFFF);
  uint32 C = (uint32)(x.lo >> 32);
  uint32 D = (uint32)(x.lo & 0xFFFFFFFF);
  uint128 y;
  y.hi = ((uint64_t)B << 32) | A;
  y.lo = ((uint64_t)D << 32) | C;
  return y;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to permute eight 16‑bit lanes within 128‑bit? 
- What tests validate boundary conditions (e.g., when A/B/C/D are all zeros or all ones)?

## Why This Question Is Asked
This question tests advanced bit manipulation skills. A palindrome check requires full 128-bit reversal and a single branchless comparison. It assesses understanding of endianness, cross-lane bit operations, and how to compose a complex operation from a reusable primitive.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit bit reversal (SWAR-style steps)
- Branchless comparison via bitwise XOR
- Endian-agnostic bit ordering across two 64-bit lanes

## Code Example
```c
int isBitPalindrome128(uint128 x) {
    uint128 r = reverseBits128(x);
    uint128 d = x ^ r;
    return (d == 0) ? 1 : 0;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to implement a hardware-style popcount without loops or built-in functions, demonstrating understanding of bitwise manipulation techniques.

## Key Concepts
- Parallel bit counting using bitmasks
- 32-bit unsigned arithmetic with logical shifts
- Elimination of branches and loops for performance

## Code Example
```javascript
function countBits32(n) {
  n = n - ((n >>> 1) & 0x55555555);
  n = (n & 0x33333333) + ((n >>> 2) & 0x33333333);
  n = (n + (n >>> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F;
  n = n + (n >>> 8);
  n = n + (n >>> 16);
  return n & 0x3F;
}
```

