## Why This Is Asked
Tests bitboard reasoning, edge handling, and efficient parallelism using only bitwise ops.

## Key Concepts
- Bitboard edge wrapping and file masks
- Parallel neighbor computation via multiple shifts
- Safe, branch-free bitwise design

## Code Example
```c
uint64_t neighborMask64(uint64_t board) {
    const uint64_t notA = 0xfefefefefefefefeULL;
    const uint64_t notH = 0x7f7f7f7f7f7f7f7fULL;
    uint64_t east  = (board & notH) << 1;
    uint64_t west  = (board & notA) >> 1;
    uint64_t south = board << 8;
    uint64_t north = board >> 8;
    uint64_t ne = (board & notH) >> 7;
    uint64_t nw = (board & notA) >> 9;
    uint64_t se = (board & notH) << 9;
    uint64_t sw = (board & notA) << 7;
    return east | west | south | north | ne | nw | se | sw;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for a 32-bit board layout?
- How would you unit-test edge cases (corners and edges) efficiently?

## Why This Is Asked

Parity computation over 128 bits tests the ability to design constant-time reductions using only bitwise operations and shifts, without loops or branches.

## Key Concepts
- Bitwise XOR folding across 128 bits
- Progressive reduction by shifting (64,32,16,8,4,2,1)
- Final extraction with a single bit AND

## Code Example
```javascript
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128_t;
unsigned parity128(uint128_t x) {
  uint64_t v = x.hi ^ x.lo;
  v ^= v >> 32;
  v ^= v >> 16;
  v ^= v >> 8;
  v ^= v >> 4;
  v ^= v >> 2;
  v ^= v >> 1;
  return v & 1;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend this to 256 bits while preserving the same approach?
- Can you prove the operation is constant time regardless of input bit distribution?
